**
An attacker develops a malicious Chrome extension named “Weather Plus” that requests OAuth scopes for Google, Microsoft, or Facebook login. During installation, the extension injects a background script (`background.js`) that opens a hidden iframe to `https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth?client_id=<attacker_id>&redirect_uri=chrome-extension://<ext-id>%2Fpopup.html&response_type=token&scope=email%20profile&state=1234` using `window.open()`. The OAuth server returns an access token via URL fragment (`#access_token=ya29...`), which is captured by a listener in the extension’s `popup.html`:
```javascript
window.addEventListener("hashchange", () => {
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({token: window.location.hash.split("#")[1]});
});
```
The token is exfiltrated via WebSocket to an attacker-controlled C2 over domain fronting to `cdncloud[.]com`. To bypass detection, the extension uses `chrome.declarativeNetRequest` to block Google’s logout endpoint and `chrome.cookies` API to steal session cookies. This technique was observed in 2024 during a campaign targeting Ukrainian NGOs, where extensions with over 100,000 downloads were used to harvest OAuth tokens for Google Workspace and Microsoft 365. Mitigation includes enforcing the `strict_oauth2` Content Security Policy and using Google’s OAuth token binding.

